Companion to `tiers-condition-tie-pseudo-class.t`: that test shows a
CROSS-block case (two different modules disagree on `:focus`/`:hover`
order; the fixed priority decides for both). This test isolates the SAME
`:focus`/`:hover` pair inside ONE file - no cross-file tie to resolve at
all - to check that the fixed condition-priority sort applies there too:
the fixed pseudo-class order holds even inside one block, not just across
blocks (see `documents/how-merging-works.md`'s "Sheet order").

One binding, one block: `:focus { color: blue }` written first,
`:hover { color: red }` written second - plain CSS, one stylesheet, no
other file in the picture. Before this change, `sort_by_shorthand_first`'s
residual "whichever came first" tie-break just replayed this file's own
declaration order (there is only one file, so no cross-file tie applies),
giving `hover` (written last) the win - the plain-CSS reading. The fixed
priority key overrides that: `:focus` (150) outranks `:hover` (130)
regardless of which one this block itself wrote last, so `:focus` is
always textually last and always wins, even in this single, unambiguous
block.

  $ cat > single_block.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "._a_2focusclr:focus{color:blue;}"]
  > [@@@css "._a_1hoverclr:hover{color:red;}"]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate single_block.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_1hoverclr:hover{color:red;}
  ._a_2focusclr:focus{color:blue;}

For an element that is BOTH hovered and focused: `color: blue` (focus)
wins - REVERSED from plain CSS's "last declaration wins" reading of this
one block (which would give `red`, hover). This is the accepted trade-off
of a global, fixed condition-priority sort (decided 2026-09-29, not
inherited as a side effect): a pseudo-class's priority is the same in
every block, in every file, so two blocks never disagree on which one
wins when both reach one element - at the cost of a single, isolated
block's own declaration order for two conditions of equal specificity.
Document this as a rule for authors: to override `:hover` with `:focus`
(or any lower-priority pseudo with a higher one), the higher-priority
pseudo always wins regardless of source order; there is no way to write
the opposite in one block anymore.
